\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{Incidence and the exterior derivative}
\date{2026-11-08}


\p{The scalar potential #{\varphi} is a field on the vertices: one value per
site, added and scaled pointwise as in the [vertex-field module](fdip-0010).
Along an edge the difference between the two endpoint values is what the edge
carries, so the [ring-valued incidence map](fdip-00AQ) sends #{\varphi} to
#{d\varphi}. The scalar-potential contribution to the electric field on the
edges is the negative of that difference. The electric field in
\ref{fdip-00AA} also subtracts #{\partial_t A}. An electrostatic
representation is one in which that derivative vanishes. A static electric
field still permits potentials with #{\partial_t A} nonzero. The gauge function #{\chi} in
#{A\mapsto A+d\chi} is another field of the same kind. On a cubic lattice
whose steps are #{x\to x+\mu}, that difference is
#{\varphi(x+\mu)-\varphi(x)}.}

\p{In the forms notation #{\varphi} is a #{0}-form and #{d\varphi} is a
#{1}-form. If the edges are oriented curves #{\gamma_e} in a manifold, running
from #{s(e)} to #{h(e)}, integration along each edge gives
##{\int_{\gamma_e} d\varphi=\varphi(h(e))-\varphi(s(e)).}
The identity is exact. The incidence map is what #{d} on #{0}-forms becomes
after that integration, and it is not #{d}: an abstract carrier has no such
curves, only this step from vertex fields to edge fields is defined, and
without faces there is no next map. A [field pullback](fdip-00AQ) is
composition with a map of the two finite sets, as in the substitution
#{f'(x)=f(h(x))}.}
